WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:02.240
Want to see something really scary?

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:06.460
The horror genre is especially fertile ground
for Easter eggs, because it's always unnerving

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.760
to discover something hiding in plain sight.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.940
Here are a few particularly ominous Easter
eggs hidden in some of your favorite horror

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:13.440
films.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:14.540
Potential spoilers ahead!

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:20.360
Many people consider 1973's The Exorcist to
be one of the finest horror films ever made.

00:00:20.369 --> 00:00:24.670
Sure, some of the scares and effects might
not pack the same punch they once did…particularly

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:26.600
after decades of parody and imitations.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:31.400
Nevertheless, The Exorcist still manages to
unnerve audiences all these years later.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.980
To add to the unease, director William Friedkin
included all sorts of subliminal sounds and

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:36.480
images.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.980
Speaking with Entertainment Weekly, he discussed
layering sounds deep into the film's mix,

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.380
like the buzzing of bees.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.280
Friedkin hoped such these effects would trigger
an unconscious fight-or-flight response in

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:56.780
the viewer.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.379
To that end, Friedkin also inserted a frightening
face into a few shots, and they're just fast

00:01:01.379 --> 00:01:04.080
enough for you to register that something
freaky is afoot.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.000
Sure, the face looks a lot less impressive
once you get a close look at it…but thanks

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.820
to some stealth editing, it's an admittedly
creepy effect.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:16.000
John Carpenter's The Thing features some impressive
practical creature effects and a slow burn

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.360
plot that still has people questioning the
ending.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:23.040
This '80s cult classic stars Kurt Russell
as a helicopter pilot fighting against a shapeshifting

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.470
alien on a remote base camp in Antarctica.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.780
Yes, it's as awesome as it sounds.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:31.340
The thrills come from watching the dwindling
research team try to determine which of their

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.420
supposed peers is actually the creature.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.060
That's no small feat, considering the alien
can mimic anything it touches.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.350
Here's the thing, though: If they'd known
how to speak Norweigan, the team could've

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.300
avoided their predicament altogether.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.600
At the beginning of the film, a dog runs towards
the team, and a clearly traumatized man is

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:47.939
trying to shoot the creature.

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:51.990
He's passionately shouting something, but
it's not in a language that any of the researchers

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:52.580
understand.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:02:00.740
[speaking Norwegian]

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.860
According to some translations, the man is
shouting something along the lines of:

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.000
"Get the hell outta there.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.660
That's not a dog, it's some sort of thing!

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.520
It's imitating a dog, it isn't real!

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.700
Get away, you idiots!"

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:14.760
They probably wouldn't have believed him anyway...but
it's still rather chilling to know the plot

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.240
is all laid out in the film's opening moments.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.520
The 2004 horror film Saw totally cleaned up
at the box office, earning almost 100 times

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.400
its $1.2 million budget.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.570
The reveal of Jigsaw's identity is a real
shocker, but eagle-eyed fans could've figured

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.500
it out much sooner if they noticed one subtle
detail.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.960
Early on in the film, we meet one of Dr. Gordon's
cancer patients, a fellow by the name of John

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:36.460
Kramer.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.560
At one point, we see Kramer resting in his
hospital bed, and savvy viewers might have

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.420
noticed an intriguing drawing on a page of
his sketchbook.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:47.420
Yes, it's a drawing of the infamous "reverse
bear trap"…you know the one.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.030
The sketch is a nice touch that's sure to
appeal to obsessive horror fans.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.340
Life is all about enjoying the little things...isn't
it?

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.800
"Most people are so ungrateful to be alive."

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:12.140
Pennywise the Dancing Clown is creepy no matter
how you slice it, both in Stephen King's gargantuan

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.230
horror novel It, and in the stellar two-part
film adaptation.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:19.820
In fact, the movie includes one particularly
chilling detail from King's novel: Pennywise

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.960
can change the color of his eyes at will.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.080
Considering all the other things he's capable
of, like, you know, transforming into a physical

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:30.530
manifestation of your greatest fear, this
might not seem like such a big deal.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.160
It's why he does it that really makes your
skin crawl.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:38.300
At the beginning of It: Chapter One, Georgie
has a famously ill-fated encounter with Pennywise…and

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:41.870
you might have noticed that the clown's eyes
suddenly change from yellow to blue.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:44.160
That comes directly from a passage of King's
novel:

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:48.300
"There were yellow eyes in there: the sort
of eyes he had always imagined but never actually

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:49.640
seen down in the basement."

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.500
A few paragraphs later, King writes,

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.160
"How, George wondered, could I have thought
his eyes were yellow?

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.440
They were a bright, dancing blue, the color
of his mom's eyes."

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.650
Pennywise realizes Georgie is about to run
away, so he changes the color of his eyes

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:05.140
to mirror Georgie's loved ones, therefore
putting the boy at ease.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.420
How insidious of him.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:07.600
"Popcorn!

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:08.780
Is that your favorite?"

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:09.500
"Uh huh."

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.600
"Mine too.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:13.780
Because they pop."

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.780
The Conjuring 2 plays a fun game of peek-a-boo
throughout the film: the demon's name Valak

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:23.900
is subtly sprinkled throughout various scenes,
always hidden somewhere in the background.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.250
For instance, it's written here on the kitchen
wall.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.950
The 'V' comes from the "Love" knickknack sitting
on top.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:32.940
It's also here, on the bookshelf, there are
five wooden letters spread out across the

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:33.440
shelves.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.980
The next time you check out The Conjuring
2, you're cordially invited to play a game

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:38.580
we like to call "Where's Valak?"

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:42.560
For instance, you can also see the name spelled
out in the beaded necklace Lorraine's daughter

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:43.060
is making.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:45.140
Let's see if you can spot it.

